1.124.m:
Brahmā said:
I shall tell you again, in Diṇḍi, about the sun's foremost and chief attendant;
he who, plunged [in sin], stood beyond the sky—even he, Rudra, is released here as Diṇḍin.

The verbal root *rājṛ* is handed down in the sense of shining, and the suffix *na* belongs to it;
because he always shines through being the general of the divine army,
therefore Kārttikeya is known by the name Rājña.

Kubera is to be known as Dhanada; Vināyaka has the form of an elephant.
Cursed by [someone] angered by contempt, a deformed body came into being;
Kubera is called Dhanada by name because of having a deformed body.

1.125.m:
Āditya and Soma—these two are disk-planets;
among them Rāhu is the shadow-planet, and the remaining are remembered as star-planets.
Soma is the lord of the asterisms, and the sun is the king of the planets;
Āditya is recited as fire, and the Moon is remembered as water.

1.130.m:
To the right side of the temple should be the bathhouse of the sun;
the auspicious agnihotra chamber should be made to the north of the sun.
Facing north should be the temple of Śambhu and of the Mothers.

